        <h1>LeetCode 678: Valid Parenthesis String (C++)</h1>
        <div class="problem-description">
            <p><strong>Problem:</strong>
                Given a string s containing only three types of characters: '(', ')', and '*', return true if
                s is valid. A string is valid if any left parenthesis has a corresponding right parenthesis,
                any right parenthesis has a corresponding left parenthesis, left parenthesis must go before
                the corresponding right parenthesis, and each '*' could be treated as a single right
                parenthesis, a single left parenthesis, or an empty string.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong>
                Input: s = "(*))"<br>
                Output: true<br>
                Explanation: Treating the '*' as a left parenthesis balances the string into a valid
                sequence.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-container">
            <button class="copy-button" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy Code</button>
            <pre><code class="language-cpp">class Solution {
public:
    bool checkValidString(string&amp; s) {
        int length = s.size();
        int minBalance = 0;
        int maxBalance = 0;

        for (int idx = length - 1; idx &gt;= 0; idx--) {
            char current = s[idx];

            minBalance += (current == ')') - (current == '(') - (current == '*');
            maxBalance += (current == ')') - (current == '(') + (current == '*');

            if (maxBalance &lt; 0) {
                return false;
            }

            minBalance = max(minBalance, 0);
        }

        return minBalance == 0;
    }
};</code></pre>
